              "text": "Depending on the context, the name **Catherine McCoy** (often spelled **Katherine McCoy**) can refer to several notable individuals:\n\n### 1. Katherine McCoy (born 1945) \u2013 American Graphic Designer and Educator\nIf you are looking for the most widely known professional by this name, it is **Katherine McCoy**. She is a highly influential American graphic designer and design theorist. \n* **Cranbrook Academy of Art:** Alongside her husband, Michael McCoy, she co-chaired the graduate Design program at the prestigious Cranbrook Academy of Art from 1971 to 1995. Under their leadership, Cranbrook became a hotbed for Postmodern design, deconstruction, and experimental graphic design.\n* **Career:** Early in her career, she worked for major firms like Unimark International (with Massimo Vignelli) and Omnigraphics (collaborating with Muriel Cooper on MIT Press projects). \n* **Distinctions:** She was the first female president of the Industrial Designers Society of America (IDSA) and was awarded the prestigious AIGA Medal in 1999 for her immense contributions to design education and practice. \n\n### 2. Catherine McCoy (1799\u20131843) \u2013 Pitcairn Island Historical Figure\nIn history and genealogy, Catherine McCoy was one of the early generation of Pitcairn Islanders.\n* **Lineage:** She was the daughter of William McCoy, a Scottish sailor who was one of the mutineers on the famous *HMAV Bounty*, and his Tahitian consort Teio. \n* **Family:** She married Arthur Quintal I (also a descendant of a *Bounty* mutineer), who served as the magistrate of Pitcairn Island. Her descendants eventually made up a significant portion of the population that relocated to Norfolk Island, making her a key figure in South Pacific ancestry and history.\n\n### 3. Other References\n* **Fictional Character:** In the 2015 comedy-drama film *Chocolate City*, **Katherine McCoy** is a character played by actress Vivica A. Fox.\n* **Longshorewoman Lawsuit:** In the mid-2000s, an American longshorewoman named **Catherine McCoy** made news headlines after winning a major $1.2 million retaliation lawsuit against the Pacific Maritime Association.",
